      "question": "Something may have spiked in Transformer 1's oil at MAIN recently, and I'd like it checked properly rather than by eye. Screen acetylene, co and hydrogen between 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000 for anomalous samples. For each channel, list any flagged timestamps with their values, and give the total number of anomalies per channel. Then tell me whether flagged points on different gases fall on the same timestamp. If acetylene and hydrogen are both flagged at the same timestamp, treat it as possible arcing and recommend an immediate confirmatory resample. If only co is flagged, treat it as possible paper involvement and recommend a routine retest. If nothing is flagged, say the window looks stable. Use a proper anomaly detection model, not a fixed threshold. If the detector can't run because a channel has fewer than 4 non-null samples, report that channel's min, max and mean instead, and flag any sample more than 50% above that channel's mean as a manual outlier.",
      "answer": "Status: I've pulled the data and searched for a way to run the detector, but hit a tooling wall \u2014 I'll summarize what I found and what I couldn't do.\n\n**Transformer 1 (MAIN) \u2014 DGA anomaly screen, 2024-01-15 \u2192 2024-01-19**\n\n**Data in window:** 4 daily samples (Jan 15, 16, 17, 18), all three channels fully populated (4 non-null each).\n\n| timestamp | acetylene | co | hydrogen |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2024-01-15 | 7 | 32 | 2845 |\n| 2024-01-16 | 6 | 30 | 2950 |\n| 2024-01-17 | 8 | 35 | 2800 |\n| 2024-01-18 | 7 | 33 | 2900 |\n\n**Model-based detection \u2014 could not be run (tooling, not data).**\nEvery channel has \u22654 samples, so your \"fewer than 4\" fallback condition is *not* met. The detector (`run_recipe`, task `tsfm_anomaly_detection`, SubLOF) requires a materialized file pointer for the series, and no tool to materialize IoT data into a file is exposed in this session (`materialize_iot` is referenced by the server but unavailable to me). I probed the TSFM work directory for an existing materialized file and found none. The catalog also has zero registered anomaly-detection model cards, so an inline SubLOF estimator would have been used. I'm not going to fabricate a detector result.\n\nNote: earlier today an unattributed SubLOF run on this asset (run:d8322fcb43, window_size=4, 4 observations) flagged index 1 (= 2024-01-16), but its record doesn't say which channel it screened, so I can't attribute it to any gas.\n\n**Manual outlier screen (your fallback rule: >50% above channel mean)**\n\n| channel | min | max | mean | 50%-above threshold | flagged | count |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| acetylene | 6 | 8 | 7.00 | 10.50 | none | 0 |\n| co | 30 | 35 | 32.50 | 48.75 | none | 0 |\n| hydrogen | 2800 | 2950 | 2873.75 | 4310.63 | none | 0 |\n\n**Cross-channel coincidence:** no flagged points on any gas, so no shared timestamps.\n\n**Conclusion:** the window looks stable \u2014 day-to-day variation is within \u00b15% on every channel, with no spike in acetylene, CO or hydrogen.\n\nTwo caveats worth acting on regardless:\n- Hydrogen is sitting at ~2,850\u20132,950 ppm on every sample. That's not a spike, but it is well above typical IEC 60599 concern levels (~150 ppm) as a sustained level \u2014 a persistent condition, not a transient, and worth a full Rogers-ratio interpretation on a fresh sample if that hasn't been done.\n- If you want the LOF-based result rather than the manual screen, expose the materialize step (or hand me a file path for the series) and I'll rerun it immediately.",
